::PC_U01_L05_GP01::
Question ID: PC_U01_L05_GP01
MLA Standard: MLA.PC.FA.11
Question:
What is the inverse of f(x) = x + 7
{
~A. f(x) = x + 7#This repeats the original function instead of undoing the addition.
~B. f(x) = 7x#This changes addition into multiplication, which does not undo x + 7.
=C. f(x) = x - 7#Correct. Subtracting 7 undoes adding 7.
~D. f(x) = x / 7#Division by 7 would undo multiplication by 7, not addition of 7.}

::PC_U01_L05_GP02::
Question ID: PC_U01_L05_GP02
MLA Standard: MLA.PC.FA.11
Question:
If f(3) = 10 and f has an inverse, what must f(10) equal
{
~A. 7#This subtracts the numbers but does not use inverse meaning.
=C. 3#Correct. An inverse reverses the ordered pair, so output 10 maps back to input 3.
~B. 30#This multiplies the numbers but does not reverse the function relationship.
~D. 10#This keeps the output as the output instead of reversing input and output.}

::PC_U01_L05_GP03::
Question ID: PC_U01_L05_GP03
MLA Standard: MLA.PC.FA.11
Question:
Which composition verifies that g is the inverse of f
{
~A. f(x) - g(x) = x#Subtraction does not show that the functions undo each other.
~B. f(x) + g(x) = 0#Adding functions does not verify inverse functions.
~C. f(x)g(x) = 1#Multiplying outputs does not verify inverse functions.
=D. f(g(x)) = x and g(f(x)) = x#Correct. Both compositions returning x verifies inverse functions.}

::PC_U01_L05_GP04::
Question ID: PC_U01_L05_GP04
MLA Standard: MLA.PC.FA.12
Question:
Why does f(x) = x² need a domain restriction to have an inverse function
{
=B. It is not one-to-one over all real numbers.#Correct. Inputs such as -2 and 2 produce the same output, so the full function is not invertible.
~A. It has no graph.#The function has a graph; the issue is failing the horizontal line test.
~C. It has no outputs.#The function has outputs, but some outputs come from two inputs.
~D. It is linear.#The function is quadratic, not linear.}

::PC_U01_L05_GP05::
Question ID: PC_U01_L05_GP05
MLA Standard: MLA.PC.FA.13
Question:
A function converts dollars to euros. What would its inverse represent
{
~A. Dollars plus euros#An inverse reverses a conversion, not adds quantities.
~B. Euros squared#Squaring is not the reverse of currency conversion.
~C. Dollars converted to dollars#The inverse changes the output unit back to the original input unit.
=D. Euros converted back to dollars#Correct. The inverse reverses the original dollars-to-euros conversion.}
